<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
  <h2>2026 Transfer Ratio Split: Reserve (1:1) vs. Preferred (4:3)</h2>
  <p>In 2026, Chase introduced card-tier differentiation for World of Hyatt transfers. Understanding which ratio applies to your card completely changes the math of whether transferring beats booking with cash or through the Chase Travel portal:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1:1 Ratio (1.00 Hyatt point per Chase point)</strong>: Retained by <strong>Chase Sapphire Reserve®</strong> and <strong>Chase Sapphire Reserve for Business℠</strong> cardholders. A 15,000-point Category 4 night requires 15,000 Chase points.</li>
    <li><strong>4:3 Ratio (0.75 Hyatt points per Chase point)</strong>: Applied to <strong>Chase Sapphire Preferred®</strong> and <strong>Ink Business Preferred®</strong> accounts (June 15, 2026 for new accounts; October 1, 2026 for existing accounts). That same 15,000-point Hyatt night requires <strong>20,000 Chase points</strong> (because <code>15,000 ÷ 0.75 = 20,000</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="formula-box" style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding: 1.25rem; background: var(--ink-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 6px;">
    <div><strong>Transferable Chase Points</strong> = Floor(Total Points ÷ 1,000) × 1,000 (minimum 1,000)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Hyatt Points Received</strong> = Transferable Points × Card Ratio (1.0 or 0.75) × (1 + Bonus %)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Points Remaining in Chase</strong> = Total Points − Transferable Points</div>
  </div>

  <h2>Worked Examples: Cash Rate vs. Chase Point Value (CPP)</h2>
  <p>Because World of Hyatt waives resort fees on full-points award stays, comparing the all-in cash price (taxes and mandatory resort fees included) against the Chase points required gives your real return:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Example 1 — Category 4 City or Resort Stay (15,000 Hyatt points/night):</strong> Suppose the total cash rate with taxes and a $40 resort fee is <strong>$360</strong> ($0 out-of-pocket fees on the Hyatt points booking):
      <ul>
        <li><em>With Sapphire Reserve (1:1):</em> Requires <strong>15,000 Chase points</strong> → <code>($360 ÷ 15,000) × 100</code> = <strong>2.40¢ per Chase point</strong>.</li>
        <li><em>With Sapphire Preferred (4:3):</em> Requires <strong>20,000 Chase points</strong> → <code>($360 ÷ 20,000) × 100</code> = <strong>1.80¢ per Chase point</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Example 2 — 1,000-Point Increment Rule (52,400 Chase points on Preferred 4:3):</strong> Only <strong>52,000 Chase points</strong> can transfer (400 points stay in your Chase balance). At the 4:3 ratio, 52,000 × 0.75 credits <strong>39,000 Hyatt points</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When NOT to Transfer Chase Points to Hyatt</h2>
  <p>Despite Hyatt's reputation as a high-value partner, transferring Chase points is not automatically optimal—especially on a 4:3 card:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Low-Cash-Rate Economy or Category 1–3 Stays:</strong> If a Category 2 hotel costs $135 all-in or 8,000 Hyatt points (1.69¢/Hyatt pt), on a 4:3 card you must spend 11,000 Chase points (rounded to 1,000s), netting only <strong>1.23¢ per Chase point</strong>—and giving up elite qualifying spend credits and 10%+ cash booking rewards.</li>
    <li><strong>When Chase Travel Points Boost Beats Partner Value:</strong> Eligible cards can access promotional Points Boost rates (up to 1.5¢–2.0¢/pt) on select portal bookings without relying on standard-room Hyatt award inventory, while paid Hyatt stays booked directly still earn World of Hyatt base points and elite night credits (check portal vs. transfer with our <a href="/en/calculators/points-vs-cash/">Points vs Cash Calculator</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Speculative Transfers Without Confirmed Dates:</strong> Never transfer Chase points to Hyatt "just in case." Points held inside Chase can still be redeemed for 1.0¢ cash back or transferred to United or Hyatt later; once sent to Hyatt, they lose multi-partner flexibility.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Check Award Room Availability First (Irreversible Policy)</h2>
  <p>Under Chase Ultimate Rewards program terms, all partner point transfers are <strong>strictly final and cannot be reversed or refunded back to Chase</strong>. Before initiating any transfer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Log into Hyatt.com and confirm that a <strong>Standard Room award</strong> (or eligible Suite award) is bookable for your exact dates and guest count.</li>
    <li>Verify that the Hyatt account name matches the Chase primary cardholder name (or authorized user name if eligible).</li>
    <li>Allow sufficient buffer if your booking is urgent: while transfers usually post within minutes, Chase officially quotes up to 7 business days in the event of a security review.</li>
  </ul>

